91:The **universe** is every own prop the checker finds on an exported function whose only parameter, or `props` parameter, is the props type, and which can be a component. React's inherited DOM attributes, `ref` and `key` are not in it. The checker has no notion of a component, so a function is left out when its shape says it is none, by the rules that clean the reference for recall (the table above): more than half of its props are declared in TypeScript's `lib.*.d.ts` (the parameter is an `Element` or a `String`), its name is a hook's, its only props are a context Provider's `value` and `children`, its name starts lowercase and is no tag's (not the file's or directory's name, no `@types/react` prop), or every prop is a `Promise`. The rules are one definition: `apps/oracle/src/component-shape.json` holds the patterns, `apps/oracle/src/component-shape.ts` applies them to the checker's functions, `scripts/weekly.py` `exclusion` to RDT's entries, and both run the cases of `apps/oracle/tests/fixtures/component-shape-cases.json`, so they cannot drift apart. A function Caliper itself reports as a component stays in the universe whatever its shape. A default export is named from its declaration (`export default function Foo` is `Foo`; one with no name of its own takes its file's, PascalCased, as Caliper names it), which is how it meets Caliper's and RDT's entries. The universe still holds some functions that are not components, because the rules read shape and not intent, so read the shares as a comparison between tools, not as a share of real components. For each tool the record has, in `oracle.tools`:
93:- **Agreement of the whole universe** (`agree_universe`): identical or spelled, over every prop in the universe. A prop the tool omits counts against it. This is the comparison between tools; Caliper's own headline is below.
99:**What changed on 2026-11-10 (`oracle.universe_version` 2).** Until then the universe counted every such function, and a default export was keyed `default`. On the 2026-11-09 weekly, whole-universe agreement on react-spectrum read Caliper 30.7% against RDT 42.5% of 5,582 props, which read as Caliper being less complete. It was two artifacts, both measured on the saved dump of that run (`apps/oracle/tests/spectrum-universe.test.ts`):
104:With the shape rules themselves over the same dump (`default` kept, as it is named now), 4,718 props: Caliper 36.3%, RDT 36.0%. The rules take out 730 props of lib-typed parameters, 58 of hooks and 76 of lowercase helpers, and keep the 10 props of lowercase functions that carry their file's name. The rest of the gap to 81.8% is the default exports, which are in the universe now.
106:`oracle.shared_props`, `agree` and `wrong` keep their meaning, over the props both tools list. Naming default exports lets Caliper's `Foo` meet the checker's `Foo`, so a corpus with default-exported components, spectrum above all, has more shared props than before (2,023 in the record of 2026-11-06); no other change reaches them. A record without `universe_version` is version 1: its `tools` count every function, with a default export named `default`. A record of version 2 keeps the scores over every function, default exports named, in `oracle.tools_all_functions`, and counts what was left out in `oracle.universe_excluded` (functions and props, by rule). The summary prints both, and the weekly does not read a rise in wrong types, or any other number of the oracle, against a record of another version.
108:A local run on antd and shadcn at the commit that added the whole-universe comparison, over every function (version 1), with the default collapse (the weekly record collapses nothing, and reads 44.7% and 90.4% for Caliper), reads, for the whole universe: Caliper 39.7% and 40.1% (its `collapsed` layers and any component it does not report count against it), RDT 44.8% and 84.8%, react-docgen 24.6% and 1.9%. This comparison between tools is information, never a gate, and the rules were validated on antd and shadcn; the weekly run produces all five corpora.
142:The sample is every prop a `--list` file names (`[{ corpus, file, component, prop }]`, for example the props two modes differ on) plus a seeded control sample, 100 per corpus by default, stratified so it is not all `string` and `boolean`. A prop falls in the first of these it fits: listed, generic, mapped (`Pick`, `Omit`, `Partial`, …), intersection, function, union of literals, from a dependency, an `@types/react` attribute (at most three per component), from another project file, own. With a tool's output given, the control sample is drawn from the components the tool reports, because the checker takes any exported one-parameter function for a component; pass `allComponents` in the config to draw from all. The candidates are capped at `universeCap` (8,000 by default), taken from the corpus's files in a seeded order, so a corpus larger than that is sampled from part of it; the number collected is printed. A listed prop the checker cannot find (a wrong component name, a file not in the list, a prop it does not see) is judged nowhere, so the report names it (`listed.missing`) beside how many were requested and found, and counts the entries that name a corpus the run does not have (`listed_for_other_corpora`).

