106:`oracle.shared_props`, `agree` and `wrong` keep their meaning, over the props both tools list. Naming default exports lets Caliper's `Foo` meet the checker's `Foo`, so a corpus with default-exported components, spectrum above all, has more shared props than before (2,023 in the record of 2026-11-06); no other change reaches them. A record without `universe_version` is version 1: its `tools` count every function, with a default export named `default`. A record of version 2 keeps the scores over every function, default exports named, in `oracle.tools_all_functions`, and counts what was left out in `oracle.universe_excluded` (functions and props, by rule). The summary prints both, and the weekly does not read a rise in wrong types, or any other number of the oracle, against a record of another version.
108:A local run on antd and shadcn at the commit that added the whole-universe comparison, over every function (version 1), with the default collapse (the weekly record collapses nothing, and reads 44.7% and 90.4% for Caliper), reads, for the whole universe: Caliper 39.7% and 40.1% (its `collapsed` layers and any component it does not report count against it), RDT 44.8% and 84.8%, react-docgen 24.6% and 1.9%. This comparison between tools is information, never a gate, and the rules were validated on antd and shadcn; the weekly run produces all five corpora.
142:The sample is every prop a `--list` file names (`[{ corpus, file, component, prop }]`, for example the props two modes differ on) plus a seeded control sample, 100 per corpus by default, stratified so it is not all `string` and `boolean`. A prop falls in the first of these it fits: listed, generic, mapped (`Pick`, `Omit`, `Partial`, …), intersection, function, union of literals, from a dependency, an `@types/react` attribute (at most three per component), from another project file, own. With a tool's output given, the control sample is drawn from the components the tool reports, because the checker takes any exported one-parameter function for a component; pass `allComponents` in the config to draw from all. The candidates are capped at `universeCap` (8,000 by default), taken from the corpus's files in a seeded order, so a corpus larger than that is sampled from part of it; the number collected is printed. A listed prop the checker cannot find (a wrong component name, a file not in the list, a prop it does not see) is judged nowhere, so the report names it (`listed.missing`) beside how many were requested and found, and counts the entries that name a corpus the run does not have (`listed_for_other_corpora`).

